      "id": 1,
      "text": "Unionville Village Historic District earned National Register status in 1979, preserving its 19th‑century rural charm.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.eastmarlboroughhistorical.org/history-of-unionville-pa/"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Founded as a Quaker settlement, Unionville became a lumber‑producing hub in the Bald Eagle Valley.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://centrehistory.org/article/unionville/"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1706 Henry Hayes purchased 1,100 acres from William Penn, laying the groundwork for Unionville.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.eastmarlboroughhistorical.org/history-of-unionville-pa/"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Quaker Zephaniah Underwood mapped Unionville in 1848 with his son William, christening the town.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://centrehistory.org/article/unionville/"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "By 1850 Unionville boasted a private seminary, town hall, churches, and bustling general stores.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        28,
        29
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.eastmarlboroughhistorical.org/history-of-unionville-pa/"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "The 1864 Bald Eagle Valley Railroad ran parallel to today’s US 220, with a small station where passengers boarded.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10,
